SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Save-the-Date: DHHS Partner Briefing on Feder
 al Medicaid Work Requirements and Increased Renewals
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Save-the-Date: DHHS Partner Briefing on Federal Medicai
 d Work Requirements and Increased Renewals\nSave-the-Date: DHHS Partner Br
 iefing on Federal Medicaid Work Requirements and Increased Renewals\nAug 1
 4\, 2026\nDHHS Partner Briefing on Federal Medicaid Work Requirements an
 d Increased Renewals\nWhen:\nMonday\, August 24\, 2026\, 3:00 – 4:30
  PM\nWhere:\nVirtual\, please\nregister\nin advance.\nYou are invited to
  join a Maine Department of Health and Human Services (DHHS) briefing to
  learn about plans to implement federal changes to Medicaid (in Maine\, 
 MaineCare) eligibility that will go into effect on January 1\, 2027.\nD
 uring this webinar\, the DHHS Office for Family Independence (OFI) and
  Office of MaineCare Services (OMS) will provide an overview of upcomi
 ng changes to MaineCare eligibility for Expansion Adults:\nNew Work R
 equirements\;\nCoverage renewals every 6 months instead of every 12 months
 \; and\nRetroactive coverage of 1 month instead of 3 months.\nMaineCar
 e Expansion members are adults between 19 and 64 years old who get Main
 eCare based on their income. They do not meet the Social Security disabil
 ity guidelines or have Medicare. OFI and OMS will also provide an updat
 e on planning for implementation and communications.\nWe are also pleased
  to share the\nPartner Communications Toolkit\n\, which can be found at
 \nMaineCare.gov/work\n. This toolkit includes materials you can download 
 and print or include in your digital and social media channels to reach th
 e populations in your community that will need to know more and take acti
 on to meet the new federal Work Requirements for Medicaid.\nImportant 
 Timelines to Note:\nAugust 14\, 2026:\nLaunch of\nFederal Changes\nand\nW
 ork Requirements\nwebpages\, and\nPartner Toolkit\nlaunch – first phas
 e\nAugust 24\, 2026:\nPartner briefing focused on what to know\nBeginning
  August 19\, 2026:\nOFI will send knowledge building letters and t\nhtt
 ps://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/save-date-dhhs-pa
 rtner-briefing-federal-medicaid-work-requirements-and-increased-renewals
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/save-date-
 dhhs-partner-briefing-federal-medicaid-work-requirements-and-increased-ren
 ewals
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:a5a29203f4c71d89@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260814T191151Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260814
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Continuing the Fight Against the Opioid Epide
 mic
DESCRIPTION:Hello\, this is Governor Janet Mills\, and thank you for liste
 ning.\nOver the five years leading up to 2019\, more than 1\,600 people in
  Maine died from drug overdoses. We were losing a person every day. About 
 8% of all babies in Maine were born substance-exposed—that's three each 
 day. The life-saving drug Naloxone was not generally available. There were
  few treatment beds\, and when someone was ready to get into recovery\, ev
 en fewer treatment options were available if they didn't have health insur
 ance.\nWhen I took office\, I knew that what Maine had been doing for so m
 any years just wasn't working. We needed a coordinated response that broke
  down the silos of government\, set aside the stigma\, and spoke out about
  every aspect of the opioid epidemic. I promised that we would put the ful
 l force of my Administration behind the families who lost loved ones\, the
  businesses who lost employees\, and all the communities that have been di
 minished by this public health crisis.\nSo my first executive order\, sign
 ed on my first day in office\, fulfilled the will of the people of Maine a
 nd expanded MaineCare to more than 90\,000 people. Today\, nearly 40\,000 
 people receive treatment for substance use disorder through MaineCare. Exp
 anding health insurance was a critical first step to confronting the opioi
 d crisis.\nI named Gordon Smith as my Director of Opioid Response\, and I 
 signed the second executive order with basic strategies and specific actio
 n steps. That order established a Prevention and Recovery Cabinet made up 
 of commissioners from 14 government agencies and the Attorney General's Of
 fice and the Judicial Branch.\nWe outlined key steps to preventing overdos
 es and deaths\, including making Naloxone widely available statewide\, and
  since early 2019\, we've distributed more than 870\,000 doses of Naloxone
 \, which has saved thousands of lives.\nWe then outlined steps to expand e
 ffective treatment and recovery measures\, including training 250 recovery
  coaches and funding recovery coaches in ten \nhttp://www.maine.gov/govern
 or/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-fight-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-
 08-14
URL:http://www.maine.gov/governor/mills/news/radio_address/continuing-figh
 t-against-opioid-epidemic-2026-08-14
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fd711eda9d92c8d3@role-healthcare-human-services.maine-government-feeds
